1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,354
Here is a claim about numbers you will never finish counting.

2
00:00:03,745 --> 00:00:06,157
Add the first odd number, and you get one.

3
00:00:06,548 --> 00:00:08,519
Add the next, and you get four.

4
00:00:08,910 --> 00:00:11,125
Then nine, sixteen, twenty five.

5
00:00:11,516 --> 00:00:14,496
The sum of the first n odd numbers is n squared.

6
00:00:14,887 --> 00:00:17,438
A computer can check millions of examples.

7
00:00:17,829 --> 00:00:23,081
But a proof has to explain why every example works, including the
ones we will never compute.

8
00:00:23,472 --> 00:00:26,232
How can a finite argument reach infinitely far?

9
00:00:26,623 --> 00:00:29,858
This is Part Zero of Landau Windows ACL Two.

10
00:00:30,249 --> 00:00:34,725
We are starting with Peano arithmetic: the rules behind whole number
reasoning.

11
00:00:36,733 --> 00:00:39,246
A formal system starts with a language.

12
00:00:39,690 --> 00:00:43,433
We will introduce its symbols, one at a time.

13
00:00:43,877 --> 00:00:46,837
This is zero, the starting number.

14
00:00:47,281 --> 00:00:50,289
This is S, the successor symbol.

15
00:00:50,733 --> 00:00:55,199
Successor means the next whole number; after zero comes one.

16
00:00:55,643 --> 00:00:58,212
This is plus, addition.

17
00:00:58,656 --> 00:01:02,173
This is the multiplication sign, times.

18
00:01:02,618 --> 00:01:05,862
This is equals, the equality symbol.

19
00:01:06,307 --> 00:01:10,345
Letters such as x and y are variable names.

20
00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:14,532
This is the symbol for and; both statements hold.

21
00:01:14,976 --> 00:01:18,150
This is not; it reverses a statement.

22
00:01:18,594 --> 00:01:22,277
For every means all values of the variable.

23
00:01:22,721 --> 00:01:26,215
There exists means at least one value.

24
00:01:26,659 --> 00:01:32,831
These are our basic arithmetic symbols: zero, successor, addition,
and multiplication.

25
00:01:33,276 --> 00:01:36,843
Zero is the only basic number constant in this language.

26
00:01:37,287 --> 00:01:41,376
We have not introduced separate basic symbols for one, two, or
three.

27
00:01:41,821 --> 00:01:47,459
Instead, the familiar written numerals are abbreviations for chains
of successors.

28
00:01:47,904 --> 00:01:53,044
The numbers are the values we are talking about; numerals are their
written names.

29
00:01:53,489 --> 00:01:57,575
For one, start at zero and take one successor step.

30
00:01:58,019 --> 00:02:01,252
For two, take another successor step.

31
00:02:01,696 --> 00:02:04,241
For three, take one more.

32
00:02:04,686 --> 00:02:10,644
So when we write the numeral three, we mean successor of successor
of successor of zero.

33
00:02:11,089 --> 00:02:17,190
The short numeral saves space, while the full expression uses only
our original symbols.

34
00:02:17,634 --> 00:02:20,704
Now we can combine those symbols into expressions.

35
00:02:22,767 --> 00:02:26,426
Consider the expression x plus S of y.

36
00:02:26,870 --> 00:02:30,968
It is a term, an expression whose value is a number.

37
00:02:31,413 --> 00:02:36,127
That description is abstract, so let us choose actual inputs.

38
00:02:36,571 --> 00:02:39,827
Let x be two, and let y be one.

39
00:02:40,272 --> 00:02:44,417
First substitute those values: two plus S of one.

40
00:02:44,861 --> 00:02:49,054
S of one means the next number after one, which is two.

41
00:02:49,499 --> 00:02:53,407
The expression has become two plus two, giving four.

42
00:02:53,851 --> 00:02:57,238
Now change just x, from two to five.

43
00:02:57,682 --> 00:03:01,579
The same term gives five plus two, which is seven.

44
00:03:02,023 --> 00:03:07,472
The term did not say true or false; it produced a number for the
supplied inputs.

45
00:03:07,916 --> 00:03:11,493
If we change the inputs, the output may change.

46
00:03:11,937 --> 00:03:14,651
Think of a term as a little arithmetic machine.

47
00:03:15,096 --> 00:03:18,722
Next, we will turn a number-producing expression into a claim.

48
00:03:20,767 --> 00:03:25,504
Now take the same term, and ask whether its value equals four.

49
00:03:25,949 --> 00:03:29,647
The expression x plus S of y equals four is a formula.

50
00:03:30,091 --> 00:03:35,469
A formula makes a claim, and we can evaluate its truth for
particular inputs.

51
00:03:35,913 --> 00:03:39,561
Use x equal to two, and y equal to one.

52
00:03:40,005 --> 00:03:44,032
The term gives four, so four equals four is true.

53
00:03:44,476 --> 00:03:48,550
Now use x equal to one, and keep y equal to one.

54
00:03:48,995 --> 00:03:53,199
The term gives three, so three equals four is false.

55
00:03:53,644 --> 00:03:57,010
Here is a table of truth values for a few assignments.

56
00:03:57,454 --> 00:04:02,476
With x equal to three and y equal to zero, the result is true.

57
00:04:02,921 --> 00:04:06,639
With both inputs zero, the result is false.

58
00:04:07,084 --> 00:04:13,078
The domain is infinite, so this table is a sample, not an exhaustive
list of inputs.

59
00:04:13,522 --> 00:04:18,144
A false row already refutes the claim that this formula holds for
every input.

60
00:04:18,589 --> 00:04:24,503
For other formulas, a finite table of true examples would not by
itself prove the universal claim.

61
00:04:24,947 --> 00:04:29,839
Terms produce values; formulas make claims about those values.

62
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:35,384
Let us build the symbol less than or equal to from arithmetic.

63
00:04:35,829 --> 00:04:41,361
We read x less than or equal to y as x is less than or equal to y.

64
00:04:41,805 --> 00:04:47,136
It means there is a whole number z, such that x plus z equals y.

65
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:50,721
The comparison is shorthand for this existence statement.

66
00:04:51,165 --> 00:04:54,931
Try x equal to two, and y equal to five.

67
00:04:55,376 --> 00:05:00,327
We can choose z equal to three, because two plus three equals five.

68
00:05:00,771 --> 00:05:03,272
So two is less than or equal to five.

69
00:05:03,716 --> 00:05:09,758
Equality is included: three is less than or equal to three, using z
equal to zero.

70
00:05:10,202 --> 00:05:15,070
Now reverse the first example: is five less than or equal to two?

71
00:05:15,515 --> 00:05:20,513
We would need a whole number z, such that five plus z equals two.

72
00:05:20,958 --> 00:05:25,790
No whole number works; adding a whole number cannot decrease five.

73
00:05:26,235 --> 00:05:28,024
That comparison is false.

74
00:05:28,469 --> 00:05:34,261
This is why the domain matters: z ranges over whole numbers, not
negative integers.

75
00:05:34,706 --> 00:05:40,999
We have explained the notation using addition and a quantifier,
instead of assuming a new primitive comparison.

76
00:05:43,067 --> 00:05:46,570
The symbols alone do not tell us which statements we may prove.

77
00:05:47,024 --> 00:05:51,642
We need starting rules, called axioms, and rules for drawing
conclusions.

78
00:05:52,097 --> 00:05:58,267
The successor axioms say zero is not a successor, and equal
successors come from equal numbers.

79
00:05:58,721 --> 00:06:01,061
Addition has two recursive rules.

80
00:06:01,515 --> 00:06:04,882
First, x plus zero equals x.

81
00:06:05,336 --> 00:06:12,591
Second, x plus the successor of y equals the successor of the
quantity x plus y.

82
00:06:13,045 --> 00:06:16,667
Let us compute three plus two, slowly.

83
00:06:17,121 --> 00:06:18,952
Two is the successor of one.

84
00:06:19,406 --> 00:06:24,082
Move that successor outside, leaving three plus one inside.

85
00:06:24,536 --> 00:06:28,933
One is the successor of zero, so repeat the same rule.

86
00:06:29,388 --> 00:06:32,164
Now use three plus zero equals three.

87
00:06:32,618 --> 00:06:37,364
Take two successor steps: four, then five.

88
00:06:37,818 --> 00:06:39,879
This is a finite calculation.

89
00:06:40,333 --> 00:06:43,667
To prove a statement for every input, we will need induction.

90
00:06:45,733 --> 00:06:49,018
Multiplication also has a recursive definition.

91
00:06:49,473 --> 00:06:55,348
First, x times zero equals zero: zero groups contain no objects.

92
00:06:55,803 --> 00:07:02,415
The next rule is x times S of y equals x times y plus x.

93
00:07:02,870 --> 00:07:08,988
S of y means y plus one, so the next multiplier adds one more group
of x.

94
00:07:09,442 --> 00:07:14,082
Let us fix x at three, and build groups of three objects.

95
00:07:14,536 --> 00:07:20,676
One group gives three: three times one is three times zero, plus
three.

96
00:07:21,130 --> 00:07:26,664
Two groups give six: take the previous three, and add three.

97
00:07:27,118 --> 00:07:32,033
Three groups give nine: take six, and add three.

98
00:07:32,488 --> 00:07:37,548
Four groups give twelve: take nine, and add three.

99
00:07:38,003 --> 00:07:43,355
Five groups give fifteen: take twelve, and add three.

100
00:07:43,809 --> 00:07:49,479
Each step increases the multiplier by one, and adds one entire group
of x.

101
00:07:49,933 --> 00:07:56,727
That is what x times S of y equals x times y plus x is saying.

102
00:07:57,182 --> 00:08:01,000
The rule is a precise way to build multiplication from addition.

103
00:08:03,067 --> 00:08:06,006
We want a reason that works for every whole number.

104
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:10,815
Start with a concrete claim: zero plus n equals n.

105
00:08:11,259 --> 00:08:17,739
Checking zero, one, and two only supplies examples.

106
00:08:18,184 --> 00:08:20,222
Induction asks for two things.

107
00:08:20,667 --> 00:08:24,047
First, prove the claim when n is zero.

108
00:08:24,492 --> 00:08:32,249
Second, take any whole number n, and show that if the claim holds
there, it also holds at the next number.

109
00:08:32,693 --> 00:08:40,836
Start at zero, and repeat that step: one, two, three, and onward.

110
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:44,919
We do not have to write infinitely many separate calculations.

111
00:08:45,363 --> 00:08:50,711
We need a starting case, and one justified step that works at any
position.

112
00:08:51,156 --> 00:08:54,047
Let us build those two ingredients explicitly.

113
00:08:56,100 --> 00:09:00,971
Let's prove something that looks almost too obvious: zero plus n
equals n.

114
00:09:01,371 --> 00:09:05,839
Our calculation axiom already tells us that n plus zero equals n.

115
00:09:06,239 --> 00:09:08,432
But that has zero on the other side.

116
00:09:08,832 --> 00:09:13,371
Until we have proved the relevant facts, we cannot silently swap the
inputs.

117
00:09:13,771 --> 00:09:18,275
For the base case, zero plus zero equals zero by the addition axiom.

118
00:09:18,675 --> 00:09:21,851
For the step, suppose zero plus n equals n.

119
00:09:22,251 --> 00:09:29,907
The recursive addition rule says zero plus the successor of n equals
the successor of zero plus n.

120
00:09:30,307 --> 00:09:33,851
Use the induction hypothesis inside those parentheses.

121
00:09:34,251 --> 00:09:38,162
The expression becomes the successor of n, exactly what we needed.

122
00:09:38,562 --> 00:09:41,679
Induction now gives the result for every whole number.

123
00:09:42,079 --> 00:09:46,950
A short proof has covered an infinite family, and every step has a
stated reason.

124
00:09:48,967 --> 00:09:52,397
Let us build our second induction example from the beginning.

125
00:09:52,852 --> 00:10:00,367
The odd numbers begin one, three, five, seven, nine.

126
00:10:00,821 --> 00:10:03,488
We need a short name for adding the first few.

127
00:10:03,942 --> 00:10:08,200
Write O of n for the sum of the first n odd numbers.

128
00:10:08,655 --> 00:10:11,188
Here n tells us how many terms to take.

129
00:10:11,642 --> 00:10:16,397
O of zero is zero, because we have not added any terms.

130
00:10:16,852 --> 00:10:19,764
For O of one, take just one.

131
00:10:20,218 --> 00:10:25,073
For O of two, keep that one, and add three.

132
00:10:25,527 --> 00:10:30,830
For O of three, keep one plus three, and add five.

133
00:10:31,285 --> 00:10:34,306
For O of four, append seven.

134
00:10:34,761 --> 00:10:37,758
For O of five, append nine.

135
00:10:38,212 --> 00:10:45,418
Every time the count increases by one, we keep the old sum and add
one new odd number.

136
00:10:45,873 --> 00:10:48,600
Which odd number comes next after n terms?

137
00:10:49,055 --> 00:10:53,142
After zero terms, the next odd number is one.

138
00:10:53,597 --> 00:10:58,194
After one term it is three; after two terms it is five.

139
00:10:58,648 --> 00:11:03,670
Each increase of one in n increases the next odd number by two.

140
00:11:04,124 --> 00:11:11,973
Starting from one and taking n steps of two gives one plus two times
n, or two n plus one.

141
00:11:12,427 --> 00:11:17,752
For example, after three terms, two times three plus one gives
seven.

142
00:11:18,206 --> 00:11:22,306
Now name the updated total O of n plus one.

143
00:11:22,761 --> 00:11:27,418
It contains the old sum O of n, plus that next odd number.

144
00:11:27,873 --> 00:11:34,945
That gives our recurrence: O of n plus one equals O of n plus two n
plus one.

145
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:38,370
The equation just describes adding one more term.

146
00:11:38,824 --> 00:11:43,797
With that bookkeeping in place, we can ask why the totals make
squares.

147
00:11:45,867 --> 00:11:50,864
Now we know how the sum grows: each step adds two n plus one.

148
00:11:51,318 --> 00:11:59,536
Our first few totals were one, four, nine, sixteen, twenty five.

149
00:11:59,991 --> 00:12:04,758
Those are square numbers; could this happen for every number of
terms?

150
00:12:05,212 --> 00:12:12,188
Our claim is that O of n, the sum of the first n odd numbers, equals
n squared.

151
00:12:12,642 --> 00:12:18,855
At zero, O of zero is zero, and zero squared is zero.

152
00:12:19,309 --> 00:12:23,652
For the next step, assume O of n equals n squared.

153
00:12:24,106 --> 00:12:29,212
The recurrence says the next sum is the old sum, plus two n plus
one.

154
00:12:29,667 --> 00:12:34,239
Replace that old sum by n squared, using our assumption.

155
00:12:34,694 --> 00:12:39,412
Pause at that expression: n squared plus two n plus one.

156
00:12:39,867 --> 00:12:41,879
Here is an n by n square.

157
00:12:42,333 --> 00:12:48,885
Add a row of n tiles, a column of n tiles, and one corner tile.

158
00:12:49,339 --> 00:12:55,391
The added border contains n plus n plus one tiles, which is two n
plus one.

159
00:12:55,845 --> 00:12:59,812
Together, they form a square of side n plus one.

160
00:13:00,267 --> 00:13:06,161
So n squared plus two n plus one equals the quantity n plus one
squared.

161
00:13:06,615 --> 00:13:13,152
That gives O of n plus one equals the quantity n plus one squared,
the next case of our claim.

162
00:13:13,606 --> 00:13:19,561
We have proved the starting case and the step; induction now gives
the result for every n.

163
00:13:20,015 --> 00:13:27,000
The picture used n equal to three to illustrate the general count;
the counting argument works for any n.

164
00:13:27,455 --> 00:13:33,697
A formal proof would expand the finite sum notation and justify the
algebraic identities used here.

165
00:13:35,767 --> 00:13:40,688
We have described the sum mathematically; now write the actual ACL2
function.

166
00:13:41,142 --> 00:13:45,530
Keep its meaning in view: O of n is the sum of the first n odd
numbers.

167
00:13:45,985 --> 00:13:49,827
This popup shows the complete definition from our checked teaching
book.

168
00:13:50,282 --> 00:13:55,085
Defun introduces video-odd-sum, with one input called n.

169
00:13:55,539 --> 00:14:00,873
For the natural-number inputs used here, the zero test asks whether
n is zero.

170
00:14:01,327 --> 00:14:04,709
If it is, return zero, the sum with no terms.

171
00:14:05,164 --> 00:14:09,130
The decrement operation subtracts one from n.

172
00:14:09,585 --> 00:14:12,930
The recursive call computes the sum with one fewer term.

173
00:14:13,385 --> 00:14:17,130
Then add the nth odd number: two times n minus one.

174
00:14:17,585 --> 00:14:24,161
For input four, the recursive call uses three, and the new odd term
is seven.

175
00:14:24,615 --> 00:14:30,282
So one plus three plus five, plus seven, gives sixteen.

176
00:14:30,736 --> 00:14:36,473
Each recursive call reduces the natural input, until it reaches
zero.

177
00:14:36,927 --> 00:14:41,509
ACL2 checks that this definition terminates before admitting the
function.

178
00:14:41,964 --> 00:14:45,406
Now fold the definition into its compact function name.

179
00:14:45,861 --> 00:14:50,333
Whenever that name appears, it refers to the definition we have just
examined.

180
00:14:52,400 --> 00:14:55,964
We have now seen two examples of the same induction pattern.

181
00:14:56,418 --> 00:15:00,870
Return to our first claim: zero plus n equals n.

182
00:15:01,324 --> 00:15:05,667
P of n is a short label for that particular formula.

183
00:15:06,121 --> 00:15:10,682
P of zero means zero plus zero equals zero.

184
00:15:11,136 --> 00:15:17,345
P of the next number means zero plus the quantity n plus one equals
n plus one.

185
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:24,482
In Peano arithmetic, the induction scheme supplies an induction
axiom for each chosen formula.

186
00:15:24,936 --> 00:15:28,015
The word scheme names that whole family of axioms.

187
00:15:28,470 --> 00:15:34,061
Now write our first claim in the language used by this project,
ACL2.

188
00:15:34,515 --> 00:15:43,236
ACL2 writes the operation first, inside parentheses, so zero plus n
becomes plus zero n.

189
00:15:43,691 --> 00:15:48,603
To say that this result equals n, wrap that expression in equal.

190
00:15:49,058 --> 00:15:57,730
ACL2 works with objects besides whole numbers, so include natp n,
meaning n is a whole number.

191
00:15:58,185 --> 00:16:04,227
Implies says: if n is a whole number, then the equality holds.

192
00:16:04,682 --> 00:16:12,261
Finally, defthm asks ACL2 to prove the statement; we name our
teaching theorem video-zero-plus-n.

193
00:16:12,715 --> 00:16:18,791
The variable n is understood universally; this theorem covers every
natural number.

194
00:16:19,245 --> 00:16:24,021
This simple equality can be handled by ACL2 arithmetic
simplification.

195
00:16:24,476 --> 00:16:32,406
For recursive claims, ACL2 can also use induction patterns justified
by terminating function definitions.

196
00:16:32,861 --> 00:16:35,782
Our other claim has this ACL2 statement.

197
00:16:36,236 --> 00:16:43,173
The function video-odd-sum is our ACL2 name for O of n, the sum of
the first n odd numbers.

198
00:16:43,627 --> 00:16:48,709
For every natural n, that function value equals n times n.

199
00:16:49,164 --> 00:16:54,524
Again, the natural-number hypothesis specifies the domain of the
claim.

200
00:16:54,979 --> 00:16:58,712
Both displayed teaching theorems have been checked in ACL2.

201
00:16:59,167 --> 00:17:04,248
The checker verifies the proof as well as reading the theorem
statement.

202
00:17:06,300 --> 00:17:11,164
Before moving on, here are the translation pairs we will keep using.

203
00:17:11,618 --> 00:17:23,197
For natural-number values, ACL2 lets us write ordinary numerals
directly; the PA successor chain for three corresponds to the ACL2
numeral three.

204
00:17:23,652 --> 00:17:29,233
Successor of n becomes plus one n, in parentheses.

205
00:17:29,688 --> 00:17:33,873
Addition x plus y is written plus x y.

206
00:17:34,327 --> 00:17:39,021
Multiplication x times y is written times x y.

207
00:17:39,476 --> 00:17:44,642
To assert an equality, use equal around the expressions being
compared.

208
00:17:45,097 --> 00:17:48,091
And combines the two equality tests shown here.

209
00:17:48,545 --> 00:17:51,442
Not reverses the truth of the equality test.

210
00:17:51,897 --> 00:17:57,585
Less than or equal to has the matching ACL2 comparison shown here.

211
00:17:58,039 --> 00:18:07,342
Our existence example gets a concrete witness: when x is less than
or equal to y, choose z equal to y minus x.

212
00:18:07,797 --> 00:18:10,912
Here is the recursive addition identity we used.

213
00:18:11,367 --> 00:18:16,364
And here is the multiplication identity, adding one more whole
group.

214
00:18:16,818 --> 00:18:22,709
The odd-sum recurrence keeps exactly the same old-sum and next-term
structure in ACL2.

215
00:18:23,164 --> 00:18:31,824
For universal claims, we use theorem statements with explicit
natural-number hypotheses, like the ones just shown.

216
00:18:32,279 --> 00:18:40,076
Later parts will keep the mathematical notation beside its ACL2
counterpart, and state what the variables mean.

217
00:18:42,133 --> 00:18:45,125
A language about whole numbers may sound restrictive.

218
00:18:45,516 --> 00:18:49,713
But finite objects can be represented by numbers and finite
sequences.

219
00:18:50,104 --> 00:18:54,255
A fraction can be stored as a numerator and a nonzero denominator.

220
00:18:54,646 --> 00:18:57,719
A finite list can be encoded by arithmetic data.

221
00:18:58,110 --> 00:19:02,713
A program, and a finite record of its execution, can also be
encoded.

222
00:19:03,104 --> 00:19:09,667
The representation has to be justified: we must prove that the
arithmetic operations preserve the meaning we intend.

223
00:19:10,058 --> 00:19:16,806
This is how an arithmetic proof can talk about a calculation without
treating arbitrary real numbers as primitive objects.

224
00:19:17,197 --> 00:19:23,122
Later, we will use exact rational intervals to enclose quantities
that are defined using analysis.

225
00:19:23,513 --> 00:19:26,214
An accepted numerical example is useful.

226
00:19:26,606 --> 00:19:31,591
A general theorem explaining why every permitted input works is a
further obligation.

227
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:36,452
There is one distinction to keep throughout this series.

228
00:19:36,843 --> 00:19:41,875
Expressing a statement in arithmetic does not, by itself, prove it
in arithmetic.

229
00:19:42,267 --> 00:19:45,084
We must actually derive it from the chosen rules.

230
00:19:45,475 --> 00:19:51,725
Nor does using a stronger background to describe numbers show that
every theorem about numbers needs that background.

231
00:19:52,116 --> 00:19:55,814
The intended interpretation here is the ordinary whole numbers.

232
00:19:56,206 --> 00:20:03,046
In the next part we will separate the written rules, the proofs made
from them, and the mathematical worlds where those rules hold.

233
00:20:03,438 --> 00:20:11,925
That will give us the vocabulary to compare Peano arithmetic with
stronger foundations, and to understand what a reduction of
assumptions really accomplishes.

234
00:20:13,933 --> 00:20:22,432
From here, we will build toward exact fractions and intervals,
verified finite searches, the smoothed prime counting statistic, and
the Landau bridge.

235
00:20:22,823 --> 00:20:31,252
The destination is a proof of the full reduction in ACL2, with a
precise account of its arithmetic strength and its connection to the
original mathematics.

236
00:20:31,643 --> 00:20:34,438
Every connection in that chain needs its own proof.

237
00:20:34,829 --> 00:20:37,994
The later episodes will follow what is actually proved.

238
00:20:38,386 --> 00:20:44,299
Part Zero gives us the starting point: a formal language, explicit
calculation rules, and induction.

239
00:20:44,690 --> 00:20:49,246
With those tools, a finite proof can say something about infinitely
many numbers.
